\section{The contrast-first order}{

In the default order the second colour is the orange (\verb{#e69f00}), at 2.25:1
against white. Any figure on white whose colour tells two or more groups
apart therefore fails the \code{geometry_contrast} row of \code{\link[=check_figure]{check_figure()}}. No
more than three of the eight colours pass every colour row of that audit
together.

\code{order = "contrast"} puts those three first: the brand blue, the vermillion
(\verb{#d55e00}) and the reddish purple (\verb{#cc79a7}), then the bluish green
(\verb{#009e73}) and the other four in their default order. Up to three groups
then pass every colour row, contrast against white included. They stay at
least 14.01 apart in CIEDE2000 under every vision type, 6.86 apart in
lightness and 3.06:1 against white. Four groups keep 3:1 against white, but
the bluish green and the reddish purple are 3.31 apart in lightness, which
fails \code{greyscale_separability}. Asking for more than four colours warns,
since the fifth, the orange, falls below 3:1.

Under this order, the second group is drawn in the vermillion. That is also
the accent (\code{\link[=depictr_options]{depictr_options()}}) that depictr uses for single highlights,
such as the flagged points of \code{\link[=outlier_plot]{outlier_plot()}} and the missing cells of
\code{\link[=missingness_map]{missingness_map()}}.

The order applies to the built-in palette alone. A palette set with
\code{depictr_options(palette = )} is the user's own, so it is returned in the
order it was set, with a warning. That is also how to make the order the
default for every later plot:
\code{depictr_options(palette = depictr_palette(order = "contrast"))}. The
palette is then the user's own, so asking for more than four colours no
longer warns. \code{\link[=scale_colour_depictr]{scale_colour_depictr()}} and \code{\link[=scale_fill_depictr]{scale_fill_depictr()}} take
\code{order} for a single plot.
